      "page": 10,
      "start_line": 26,
      "end_line": 47,
      "numbered_excerpt": "26|criteria. We used the LOCF method to impute for missing\n27|data, which can overestimate effi cacy;31 however, this\n28|method is a widespread method of imputation, especially\n29|in neuro pathic pain clinical trials,8 and few data were\n30|missing in the present study (appendix p 14).\n31|Furthermore, fi ndings from a post-hoc sensitivity\n32|analysis showed that the LOCF results were similar to\n33|those of three other imputation methods. Finally, there\n34|was risk of unmasking during the study period because\n35|of the eff ects of subcutaneous botulinum toxin A on skin\n36|appearance, specifi cally wrinkles or colour. We did not\n37|photograph our participants' skin during the study, but\n38|photographs of a woman who participated in this trial\n39|and was treated after the trial with subcutaneous\n40|botulinum toxin A for peripheral neuro pathic pain with\n41|the same method and doses as in the present study show\n42|no skin modifi cation (appendix p 15). Patients with facial\n43|pain were excluded from our study, because the eff ects of\n44|botulinum toxin on fi ne facial features would probably\n45|contribute to unmasking; neither the patients nor the\n46|investigators reported recognising the treatment because\n47|of skin modifi cations.",
      "numbered_excerpt_sha256": "a81b7926bda77c0834edc243b7ad10096a00e435a14560098741a9eb42a846a0",
