      "page": 4,
      "start_line": 40,
      "end_line": 63,
      "numbered_excerpt": "40|The effi cacy analyses for the primary outcome and for\n41|all but one secondary outcomes included all randomly\n42|assigned participants (intention-to-treat population)\n43|and used a last-observation-carried-forward (LOCF)\n44|imputation. Analysis of the therapeutic gain of the\n45|second injection (a secondary endpoint) included all\n46|randomly assigned participants who received two\n47|administrations of the treatment (as-treated analysis,\n48|based on the actual treatment the participants received)\n49|and used a LOCF imputation for missing values.\n50|We used a generalised linear mixed-model repeated-\n51|measures analysis to investigate the prespecifi ed primary\n52|endpoint. This model compared the mean change in\n53|weekly or daily pain ratings over time after two\n54|administrations of botulinum toxin A with that after\n55|placebo each week for 24 weeks, with baseline mean pain\n56|intensity as a covariate. We used the same models to\n57|assess the eff ects of botulinum toxin A on the secondary\n58|outcomes. In these models, treatment eff ect estimates\n59|correspond to a relative diff erence between botulinum\n60|toxin A and placebo for daily pain ratings over 24 weeks,\n61|on a mixed longitudinal model averaged across all\n62|patients and adjusted on all covariates of the model,\n63|including time.",
      "numbered_excerpt_sha256": "e4e7f4f885e658db45961e96e4800711bc9285693e1156a15ff19a0146ff2597",
